<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Valmiera</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wolmar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Valmiera (German: Wolmar) is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauja">Gauja</a> river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidzeme">Vidzeme</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>. It grew around a castle of the Livonian Order founded in 1283, became a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> and was a frequent meeting place of the Livonian Landtag. It was repeatedly fought over in the wars between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valmiera.jpg" alt="Valmiera" width="320" height="258" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nikolay Yagunov / Николай Ягунов · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:44_%D0%9B%D0%B0_%D0%92%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%BC%D0%B8%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B0.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Valmiera is the second largest city of the historical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidzeme">Vidzeme</a> region, with an area of 19.35 square kilometres. It lies on both banks of the Gauja River, at the crossroads of several important roads, 107 kilometres north-east of Riga and 50 km south of the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>. The site is one of the longest-inhabited in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, and archaeological evidence indicates it was occupied 9,000 years ago. The name may derive from the Old German given name Waldemar or the Slavic name Vladimir, and the town may have been named after Vladimir Mstislavich, prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a>, or after King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-ii-of-denmark">Valdemar II of Denmark</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the partition of Tālava in 1224 the trade roads along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauja">Gauja</a> passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a>, who built a castle that is now known as Valmiera Castle. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-saule">Battle of Saule</a> in 1236 the castle and nearby lands became the property of the newly established Livonian Order. Valmiera was first mentioned as a town in a chronicle of 1323. It belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> from the 14th to the 16th centuries, which brought significant trade, and several regional assemblies, or landtags, were held there in the same period.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a> left the town heavily devastated in 1583, and it was incorporated into the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>. After the Polish-Swedish war of 1622 it was managed by the Swedish Lord High Chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axel-oxenstierna">Axel Oxenstierna</a>. It was destroyed and burned down in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> in 1702 and then devastated by plague. In 1738 the first seminary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravian-church">Moravian Church</a> was opened there, and in 1785 Valmiera became the centre of a district. The first bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauja">Gauja</a> was built in 1865, and the Riga to St Petersburg railway reached the town in 1899, bringing new factories.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In 1906 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a> won 18 of 24 seats in the municipal elections, and the local doctor Georgs Apinis was elected mayor. In the Second World War German troops captured the town on 4 July 1941, and in October 1941 209 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from Valmiera and the surrounding area were murdered in local forests. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> recaptured it on 24 September 1944, by which time almost all the buildings at the centre had been destroyed. Valmiera is an industrial centre, with food processing, fibreglass production, metalworking and woodworking.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Valmiera” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its main institution was the Landtag, a diet usually held at Walk or <b>Wolmar</b>, where the territorial lords and estates met to decide on war, peace, coinage and trade.</blockquote><a href="/state/livonian-confederation">Livonian Confederation</a></li></ul></section>
